<section xml:id="sec-logical-values-statements2">
	<title>Logical Values &amp; Statements</title>

	<introduction>
		
		<p>
			Writing good code often means making decisions based on the values you are working with.
			For example, suppose <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> stores the amount of money on an Amazon gift card and
			<pf>total_due</pf> stores the price of a purchase. Before completing the purchase, your program needs to ask a
			yes-or-no question:
			<q>Is <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> greater than or equal to <pf>total_due</pf>?</q>
		</p>

		<p>
			Questions like this are called <term>logical statements</term>, and their answers are called
			<term>logical values</term>. In MATLAB, logical values are the two special values <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>.
			In this section, we will learn how MATLAB represents these answers, how to build questions using relational
			operators, and how to combine and negate questions to express more complicated conditions.
		</p>

	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-values">
		<title>Logical Values</title>

		<p>
			MATLAB has two logical values:
			<pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>. When displayed, they often appear as <pf>logical 1</pf> and <pf>logical 0</pf>.
			Although they behave similarly to the numeric values <pf>1</pf> and <pf>0</pf>, they are different data types.
			Just as numbers can be stored in variables, logical values can be stored in variables as well.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			a = 1
			b = true
			c = 0
			d = false
		</program>

		<p>
			Even though <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> (and <pf>c</pf> and <pf>d</pf>) look similar at first glance, MATLAB treats them differently:
			<pf>a</pf> is a number (a double), while <pf>b</pf> is a logical value. You can get more information using the <pf>whos</pf> command.
			Here is the output you should see:
		</p>

		<pre>
			Name        Size            Bytes  Class      Attributes
			a           1x1                 8  double
			b           1x1                 1  logical
			c           1x1                 8  double
			d           1x1                 1  logical
		</pre>

		<p>
			Notice that logical variables take fewer bytes than double-precision numbers. 
			That memory difference is not the main reason we use logicals, but it is a 
			helpful reminder that a logical value represents an answer, not a measurement.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-statements">
		<title>Logical Statements</title>

		<p>
			A <term>logical statement</term> is any MATLAB expression that evaluates to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.
			The most common logical statements come from comparing two values. These comparisons use
			<term>relational operators</term>.
		</p>

		<table xml:id="tab-relational-operators">
			<title>Relational operators in MATLAB</title>

			<tabular>
				<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
				<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
				<col width="50%" halign="left"/>
				<row header="yes">
					<cell>Relational operator</cell>
					<cell>Logical statement</cell>
					<cell>Question being asked</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>==</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x == y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>~=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x ~= y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> not equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&lt;</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &lt; y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&lt;=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &lt;= y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than or equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&gt;</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &gt; y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> greater than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&gt;=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &gt;= y</pf></cell>
					<cell><p><m>\text{Is}\ \texttt{x}\ \text{greater than or equal to}\ \texttt{y} \text{?}</m></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			A beginner mistake is confusing <pf>=</pf> with <pf>==</pf>.
			The single equal sign assigns a value to a variable (telling MATLAB what to store),
			while the double equal sign compares two values (asking MATLAB a question).
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			cost = 7.1;     % Telling: assign a value
			cost == 7.1     % Asking: compare a value
		</program>

		<p>
			Here are a few comparison examples. The parentheses are optional, but they help clarify
			which parts of the expression are being compared.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			a = 4; 
			b = -4;

			a_eq_4 = (a == 4);	% ← Is a equal to 4?	Yes.
			a_eq_b = (a == b);	% ← Is a equal to b?	No.
			b_lt_a = (b &lt; a);	% ← Is b less than a?	Yes.
		</program>

		<p>
			This code defines two numeric variables, <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf>, and three logical variables:

			<ul>
				<li><pf>a_eq_4 = 1 (logical)</pf> since <pf>a == 4</pf> is true.</li>
				<li><pf>a_eq_b = 0 (logical)</pf> since <pf>a == b</pf> is false.</li>
				<li><pf>b_lt_a = 1 (logical)</pf> since <pf>b &lt; a</pf> is true.</li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<exercise>
			<title>Check Your Understanding: Logical Statements</title>
			
			<task label="logic-ckpt-intro">
				<title>Logical Statements</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Let <pf>x</pf>, <pf>y</pf>, and <pf>z</pf> be numeric variables. Which of the following are valid logical statements in MATLAB?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>x = y</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>x = y</pf> uses a single equal sign, which is for assignment, not comparison.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>z &gt; y</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The expression <pf>z &gt; y</pf> is a valid logical statement that asks if <pf>z</pf> is greater than <pf>y</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>y =&lt; 2</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>y =&lt; 2</pf> uses an incorrect operator; the correct operator for "less than or equal to" is <pf>&lt;=</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>z ~= z</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The expression <pf>z ~= z</pf> is a valid logical statement that asks if <pf>z</pf> is not equal to <pf>z</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>x ~ z</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>x ~ z</pf> is not a valid logical statement because it lacks a proper relational operator.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the result of the following logical expression in MATLAB?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						p = 10 &gt; 5;
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Since 10 is indeed greater than 5, the expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf> (logical 1).
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Remember that 10 is greater than 5, so the expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>1</pf> (double) and <pf>true</pf> (logical 1) may seem similar, they are different data types in MATLAB.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf> (logical 1), not <pf>false</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the result of the following logical expression in MATLAB?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						r = (pi ~= 3.14);
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Since 3.14 is only an approximation of pi, it is not equal to pi.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>1</pf> (double) and <pf>true</pf> (logical 1) may seem similar, they are different data types in MATLAB.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>
		</exercise>


		<!-- <pre>
			Name        Size            Bytes  Class      Attributes
			a           1x1                 8  double               
			b           1x1                 8  double               
			a_eq_4      1x1                 1  logical              
			a_eq_b      1x1                 1  logical              
			b_lt_a      1x1                 1  logical              
			r           1x1                 1  logical              
		</pre> -->

		<!-- <p>
			In the <pf>whos</pf> output, notice that the variables <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> are doubles, while the others are logicals. This is because the comparison operations produce logical values.
		</p> -->

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-negating-logical-statements">
		<title>Negating Logical Statements</title>

		<p>
			To ask the opposite of a logical statement, use the NOT operator <pf>~</pf>.
			If an expression is true, its negation is false, and vice versa.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			~true	% returns false
			~false	% returns true
		</program>


		<p>
			The NOT operator negates a single logical value or statement. When the statement is a comparison, 
			use parentheses so MATLAB negates the whole comparison.
		</p>

		<p>
			For example, the following statements ask if <q>8 is not equal to 9</q>:
			<ul>
				<li><pf>~(8 == 9)</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>NOT (8 is equal to 9)</q></li>
				<li><pf>8 ~= 9</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>8 is not equal to 9</q></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>
			Similarly, the negation of <pf>x &gt;= 5</pf> is <pf>x &lt; 5</pf>, so the two statements are equivalent:
			<ul>
				<li><pf>~(x &gt;= 5)</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>NOT (x is greater than or equal to 5)</q></li>
				<li><pf>x &lt; 5</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>x is less than 5</q></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>
			When negating expressions, use parentheses to make your intent clear and improve the readability of your code.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-joining-two-statements">
		<title>Joining Two Statements</title>

			<p>
				Many questions involve multiple conditions. For example, to determine if a number is an even number greater than 100,
				then you need to check two things at once:
				<ul>
					<li>Is the number even?</li>
					<li>Is the number greater than 100?</li>
				</ul>
				Because both must be true, we join these statements with <q>and</q>. 
				If either of these conditions could be true, then we would join them with <q>or</q>. 
			</p>

			<p>
				MATLAB joins logical statements with AND and OR using the operators <pf>&amp;</pf> (AND) and <pf>|</pf> (OR). 
			</p>

			<table xml:id="tab-logical-operators">
				<title>Logical operators for combining statements</title>

				<tabular>
					<col width="15%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="40%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="45%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Logical}</m> <m>\textbf{operator}</m></p></cell>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Logical statement}</m></p></cell>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Question being asked}</m></p></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&amp;</pf></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></p></cell>
						<cell>Are both <m>\texttt{ans1}</m> and <m>\texttt{ans2}</m> true?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>|</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
						<cell><m>\text{Is at least one of}\ \texttt{ans1}\ \text{or}\ \texttt{ans2}\ \text{true?}</m></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>
				The result of joining logical statements are exactly what you would expect from everyday language:
				AND is true only when both sides are true, while OR is true when at least one side is true.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				% Quick checks
				true  &amp; true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				true  &amp; false	% → false: 0 (logical)
				false &amp; true	% → false: 0 (logical)
				false &amp; false	% → false: 0 (logical)

				true  | true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				true  | false	% → true:  1 (logical)
				false | true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				false | false	% → false: 0 (logical)
			</program>

			<p>
				Now try these operators in context.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					P = 1;
					Q = 10;
					R = -4;

					P &lt;= R &amp; P &gt; Q	  % → false	&amp; false: 0 (logical)
					R &lt; 0 &amp; R &lt; Q	  % → true	&amp; true:  1 (logical)
					Q == R | Q == P	  % → false	| false: 0 (logical)
					P &lt; Q | P &lt; R	  % → true	| false: 1 (logical)

					~(P &lt; Q &amp; P &lt; R)  % → NOT (true &amp; false): 1 (logical)
					P &gt; Q | P &gt; R	  % → false | true:		  1 (logical)
				</code>
			</program>

			<!-- <p>
				These examples are intentionally written in a style that reads like an English sentence.
				That habit pays off later: readable conditions produce readable <pf>if</pf>-statements.
			</p> -->

		<!-- <aside>
			<title><pf>xor</pf></title>
			<p><pf>xor</pf> is another logical operator available in MATLAB that tends to be used less than the operators listed here. If you are curious about it, see MATLAB's documentation.</p>
		</aside>

		<p>
			The following table summarizes every possible outcome for each of the logical operators above.
		</p>

		<table>
			<title>Logical Operators. Truth Tables. </title>

			<tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" >
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<row>
					<cell colspan="2">Logical Variables</cell>
					<cell colspan="3">Logical Operator Results</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>ans1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>~ans1</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table> -->

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-joining-many-statements">
		<title>Joining Many Statements</title>

			<p>
				You can combine more than two logical statements, and you can mix <pf>~</pf>, <pf>&amp;</pf>, and <pf>|</pf>
				in the same expression. When you do, MATLAB evaluates operators in a fixed order.
				Parentheses override everything, so you can always force the grouping you intend.
			</p>

			<p>
				In general, the logical operator precedence chain is as follows:
			</p>

			<tabular halign="center">
				<row>
					<cell>1st</cell><cell/><cell>2nd</cell><cell/><cell>3rd</cell><cell/><cell>4th</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>()</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>~</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>&amp;</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>|</pf></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<table>
				<title>Sample Logical Operator Precedence Interpretations</title>

				<tabular halign="center">
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>MATLAB Command</cell><cell/><cell>Interpretation</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>~A &amp; B</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(~A) &amp; B</pf></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>~A | B</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(~A) | B</pf></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>A &amp; B | C</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(A &amp; B) | C</pf></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>A | B &amp; C</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>A | (B &amp; C)</pf></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>
				When in doubt, use parentheses. They make your code clearer to readers and reduce errors caused by
				misinterpreting precedence.
			</p>

	</subsection>

	<exercises xml:id="logic-cqs">
		<title>🤔💭 Conceptual Questions</title>

		<exercise label="logic-ckpts">

			<task label="logic-ckpt-data-types">
				<title>Logical Data Types</title>
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						In MATLAB, the values <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> are exactly the same and can always be used interchangeably.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						While <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> appear similar and behave similarly in many contexts, they are different data types. 
						<pf>true</pf> is a logical value, while <pf>1</pf> is a numeric (double) value. 
						You can see this difference using the <pf>whos</pf> command.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-relational-equal">
				<title>Relational Operators: Equality</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which operator tests if <pf>x</pf> is equal to <pf>y</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>==</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>=</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~=</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>!=</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The double equal sign <pf>==</pf> compares two values for equality. 
						The single equal sign <pf>=</pf> is used for assignment, not comparison.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-relational-not-equal">
				<title>Relational Operators: Inequality</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which operator tests if <pf>x</pf> is not equal to <pf>y</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>~=</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>!=</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>==</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>&lt;&gt;</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						In MATLAB, the tilde-equals <pf>~=</pf> operator tests for inequality. 
						Note that many other languages use <pf>!=</pf>, but MATLAB uses <pf>~=</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-translation-intro">
				<title>Translating Relational Operators</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						For each English question below, provide the corresponding MATLAB logical statement.
						The table below shows some examples to help you understand the pattern.
					</p>

					<table>
						<title>Sample English and MATLAB translation of relational operators</title>
						<tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
							<col halign="right"/>
							<col halign="center"/>
							<col halign="center"/>
							<col halign="center"/>
							<row>
								<cell halign="center" colspan="2">English</cell>
								<cell halign="center" colspan="2">MATLAB</cell>
							</row>
							<row top="minor" bottom="minor">
								<cell halign="center" right="none">Question</cell>
								<cell>Answer</cell>
								<cell right="none">Question</cell>
								<cell>Answer</cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{4}</m> less than <m>\texttt{4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>no</cell>
								<cell><pf>4 &lt; 4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{4}</m> less than or equal to <m>\texttt{4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>yes</cell>
								<cell><pf>4 &lt;= 4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{3}</m> greater than <m>\texttt{-4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>yes</cell>
								<cell><pf>3 &gt; -4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{3}</m> greater than or equal to <m>\texttt{-4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>yes</cell>
								<cell><pf>3 &gt;= -4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{4}</m> equal to <m>\texttt{-4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>no</cell>
								<cell><pf>4 == -4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
							</row>
							<row bottom="minor">
								<cell>Is <m>\texttt{4}</m> not equal to <m>\texttt{-4}</m>?</cell>
								<cell>yes</cell>
								<cell><pf>4 ~= -4</pf></cell>
								<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
							</row>
						</tabular>
					</table>

					<p>
						What MATLAB command would you use to ask: <q>Is <m>7</m> greater than <m>3</m>?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						<fillin mode="string" answer="7 &gt; 3" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>(7\s*&gt;\s*3|3\s*&lt;\s*7)</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>&gt;</pf> operator to test if one value is greater than another.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution>
					<p>
						The MATLAB command is <pf>7 &gt; 3</pf>, which evaluates to <pf>1</pf> (true).
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-basic-ops-result">
				<title>Basic Logical Operators</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Evaluate the following logical expression in MATLAB:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							(3 &gt;= 2) &amp; (3+4 == 6)
						</code>
					</program>
					<p>
						What is the result of this expression?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (false)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Even though <pf>3 &gt;= 2</pf> is true, the expression <pf>3+4 == 6</pf> is false (since <m>3+4=7</m>). 
								For an AND operation to be true, both sides must be true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (true)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>3 &gt;= 2</pf> is true, check the second part: is <m>3+4</m> really equal to <m>6</m>?
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-basic-ops-explanation">
				<title>Understanding AND</title>
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						An AND operation (<pf>&amp;</pf>) returns true if at least one of the conditions is true.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Incorrect. An AND operation requires <em>both</em> conditions to be true. 
						You're thinking of OR (<pf>|</pf>), which returns true if <em>at least one</em> condition is true.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-combined-1">
				<title>Is p positive?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using <pf>p = -3</pf>, what command asks: <q>Is <pf>p</pf> positive?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						<fillin mode="string" answer="p &gt; 0" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
					<evaluation>
						<evaluate>
							<test correct="yes">
								<strcmp>(p\s*&gt;\s*0|0\s*&lt;\s*p)</strcmp>
								<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
							</test>
							<test>
								<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
								<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>&gt;</pf> operator to test if <pf>p</pf> is greater than 0.</p></feedback>
							</test>
						</evaluate>
					</evaluation>
					<solution>
						<p>
							<pf>ans1 = p &gt; 0</pf> evaluates to <pf>0</pf> (false) since <m>-3</m> is not positive.
						</p>
					</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-combined-2">
				<title>Is p equal to 4?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using <pf>p = -3</pf>, what command asks: <q>Is <pf>p</pf> equal to <pf>4</pf>?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						<fillin mode="string" answer="p == 4" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>(p\s*==\s*4|4\s*==\s*p)</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>==</pf> operator to test equality.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<pf>ans2 = p == 4</pf> evaluates to <pf>0</pf> (false) since <m>-3 \neq 4</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-combined-3">
				<title>Combining with AND</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using the variables <pf>ans1</pf> and <pf>ans2</pf> defined above, write a command that asks: <q>Is <pf>p</pf> positive AND equal to <pf>4</pf>?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						Use the element-wise AND operator to combine <pf>ans1</pf> and <pf>ans2</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>ans3 = ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans3 = ans1 | ans2</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans3 = ~ans1</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans3 = ans1 == ans2</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The element-wise AND operator <pf>&amp;</pf> combines two conditions, returning true only if both are true.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-combined-4">
				<title>Negating with NOT</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using the variable <pf>ans1</pf> defined above, write a command that asks: <q>Is <pf>p</pf> NOT positive?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						Use the NOT operator to negate <pf>ans1</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>ans4 = ~ans1</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans4 = ans1 &amp; 0</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans4 = !ans1</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans4 = ans1 &lt;= 0</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The NOT operator <pf>~</pf> negates a logical value, turning true into false and false into true.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-combined-5">
				<title>Combining with OR</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using <pf>p = -3</pf>, what command asks: <q>Is <pf>p</pf> not equal to <pf>5</pf> OR greater than <pf>7</pf>?</q>
					</p>
					<p>
						<fillin mode="string" answer="(p ~= 5) | (p &gt; 7)" width="20"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>\(?\s*p\s*~=\s*5\s*\)?\s*\|\s*\(?\s*p\s*&gt;\s*7\s*\)?</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use <pf>~=</pf> for "not equal" and <pf>|</pf> for OR.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<pf>ans5 = (p ~= 5) | (p &gt; 7)</pf> evaluates to <pf>1</pf> (true) since <m>-3 \neq 5</m> is true.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-precedence">
				<title>Operator Precedence</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Which of the following expressions is equivalent to <pf>~A &amp; B | C</pf> according to MATLAB's operator precedence rules?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>((~A) &amp; B) | C</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~(A &amp; B | C)</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~A &amp; (B | C)</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~(A &amp; B) | C</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						MATLAB evaluates logical operators in this order: first <pf>~</pf> (NOT), then <pf>&amp;</pf> (AND), then <pf>|</pf> (OR).
						So <pf>~A &amp; B | C</pf> is evaluated as <pf>((~A) &amp; B) | C</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-short-circuit">
				<title>Short-Circuit Operators</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						When should you use <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf> instead of <pf>&amp;</pf> and <pf>|</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>When working with scalar logical values in conditional statements (like <pf>if</pf>)</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The short-circuit operators <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf> are designed for scalar logical values 
								and are commonly used in conditional statements. They also provide short-circuit evaluation, which can prevent errors.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>When performing element-wise operations on arrays</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Use <pf>&amp;</pf> and <pf>|</pf> for element-wise operations on arrays. 
								The short-circuit operators <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf> work only with scalar values.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>They are completely interchangeable and can be used in any situation</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While they behave similarly for scalar values, <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf> do not work on arrays, 
								and <pf>&amp;</pf> and <pf>|</pf> do not provide short-circuit evaluation.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>When you want the computation to run faster</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Partially correct. Short-circuit operators can be faster because they skip unnecessary evaluations, 
								but the main reason to use them is for scalar logical expressions and to prevent potential errors in conditionals.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

		</exercise>

	</exercises>

</section>
